\chapter{Introduction}

\section{Background}

Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems combine information retrieval with
language generation. This design addresses a limit of purely parametric large
language models (LLMs), whose knowledge is fixed at training time and cannot be
updated without retraining or fine-tuning. \citet{lewis2020rag}
introduced RAG by combining a pretrained retriever with a pretrained
sequence-to-sequence generator, so the model can base its output on external and
updatable evidence. A RAG
system takes the user query, retrieves relevant information from an external
knowledge base and gives that information to a
language model with instructions for generating the final response.

The system prompt connects the intended system behaviour to the
observable output. The retrieved evidence varies with the query and the
knowledge base, but the system prompt is written to specify how
the model should behave, which instructions and constraints apply, and which
output format it should produce. Because it is written in natural language, its effect on behaviour is hard to
predict and hard to check in advance. Its influence therefore has to be examined
through controlled experiments.

\section{Motivation and Research Objective}

How a RAG system responds to its system prompt matters in practice. Whether the
system follows its instructions reliably decides whether its behaviour can be
trusted once deployed. The question is then \textit{how systematic changes in the system
prompt can be shown to alter the behaviour and outcomes of a RAG system.}

Isolating that effect can be difficult. The influence of the system prompt overlaps
with the influence of the retrieved evidence, of the language model itself, and
of the exact wording of the instructions, and all of these have to be controlled
before any change in behaviour can be assigned to the prompt. Small, seemingly harmless changes to the wording of an instruction can cause large changes in model behaviour
\citep{salinas2024butterfly}.

The central research question is the following: \\
\textit{To what extent do
systematic changes in the system prompt influence the behaviour and outcomes of a
RAG system when the user input, knowledge base, retrieval mechanism, and
underlying language model are held fixed?} \\
Answering this question requires a formal
model of the RAG system to identify the system prompt as a controlled parameter, and an experimental design that holds every other source
of variation fixed. The rest of this thesis builds both, and builds them for the simplest
retrieval-augmented architecture. In that architecture the system prompt reaches
the answer through a single path. That is what makes its effect identifiable.


\section{Thesis Structure}

This thesis has six chapters. Chapter 2 covers the background needed for the
rest of the thesis. It elaborates how retrieval-augmented approaches to language
generation developed and introduces the different components of a RAG system. Chapter 3 reviews the relevant literature and identifies the research gap. Chapter 4 builds the
formal model of the RAG system used in this thesis. It takes the probabilistic
formulation of \citet{lewis2020rag} and extends it such that the system prompt
becomes an explicit variable of the generator, grounds the knowledge base, and defines what counts as a good answer.
Chapter 5 describes the experimental setup. Chapter 6 presents and discusses the
results, summarises the findings and states what the design does not establish.
The Notation section at the end of this chapter
lists every symbol used later, so that the formal chapters can be read without
searching back for a definition.

\section*{Notation}
\label{sec:notation}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Notation}

The notation follows \citet{lewis2020rag} for the RAG system and the intervention calculus of \citet{pearl2009causality} for the causal statements. The symbols used in the later chapters are listed here.

\subsection*{The system and its components}

\begin{itemize}
    \item $q \in \mathcal{Q}$ denotes a user query, and $P_{\mathcal{Q}}$ the distribution over the query space $\mathcal{Q}$ from which queries are drawn.
    \item $D = \{d_1, d_2, \ldots, d_N\}$ denotes the knowledge base, where each $d_i$ is a document and $N$ is the number of documents.
    \item $S$ denotes a system prompt. It is the standing instruction supplied in the system role, fixed across requests, and is distinct from the query $q$ that the user supplies anew with each request.
    \item $C$ denotes the retrieved context supplied to the language model.
    \item $y = (y_1,\ldots,y_L)$ denotes the generated system output, a sequence of $L$ tokens; $y_{1:i-1}$ denotes the tokens produced before position $i$.
    \item $R$ and $G$ denote the retrieval and generation functions of the pipeline, and $\chi$ the chunking strategy by which documents are divided into retrievable units.
    \item $v \in \mathcal{V}$ denotes a token of the vocabulary $\mathcal{V}$, $u_v$ the score the generator assigns it, and $T > 0$ the decoding temperature.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{The probabilistic model}

\begin{itemize}
    \item $z \in D$ denotes a single retrieved document, and $\mathcal{Z}_K(q,D)$ the $K$ documents with the highest retrieval probability.
    \item $p_\eta(z \mid q,D)$ denotes the retrieval distribution with parameters $\eta$, and $p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S)$ the generator with parameters $\theta$.
    \item $p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S)$ denotes the distribution over outputs obtained by marginalising the generator over the retrieved documents.
    \item $Y$ denotes the output viewed as a random variable, $M(Y)$ a measured property of it, and $\varepsilon$ the randomness introduced by decoding.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{The system prompt and its variation}

\begin{itemize}
    \item $\Sigma$ denotes the set of requirements a system prompt states. Each requirement is a property an answer either has or does not have, so that it is checkable on any output.
    \item $\varphi$ denotes the wording in which those requirements are expressed, and $S = \rho(\Sigma,\varphi)$ denotes the resulting system prompt.
    \item $S_{b,0},\ldots,S_{b,3}$ denote the four prompt conditions of baseline $b \in \{A,B,C\}$, twelve in all, with $S_{b,0}$ the canonical form of that baseline. All four share the requirement set $\Sigma_b$ and differ in wording only.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Causal intervention}

\begin{itemize}
    \item $\mathrm{do}(S = S_j)$ denotes the intervention that sets the system prompt to condition $j$, as distinct from observing it to take that value.
    \item $\tau_j$ denotes the average causal effect of condition $S_j$ relative to the baseline $S_0$, taken over the query distribution $P_{\mathcal{Q}}$, and $\hat{\tau}_j$ its estimate from the sample. The effect is defined within a cell of the design, so it depends on the baseline, the measure and the evidence level as well as on $j$; only $j$ is written, and the other three are fixed by context wherever the symbol is used.
    \item $\mathbb{E}[\cdot]$ denotes expectation, and $\hat{\cdot}$ marks a quantity estimated from the sample rather than defined in the model.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Experimental design}

\begin{itemize}
    \item $c_{ik}$ denotes the $k$-th context given to query $q_i$, with $c^{\text{gold}}$ and $c^{\text{ret}}$ the two evidence levels: the annotated supporting passages, and the genuine retriever output.
    \item $\mathcal{Q}_{\text{direct}}$ and $\mathcal{Q}_{\text{multi-hop}}$ denote the two query categories, and $N_Q$ the size of the query set.
    \item $A_q$ denotes the reference answers annotated for query $q$, against which correctness is measured.
    \item $I$ denotes the set of queries in one cell of the design, and $\bar{M}_{ij}$ the mean of a measure over the $n$ repetitions of query $i$ under prompt condition $j$.
    \item The indices are $i \in \{1,\ldots,N_Q\}$ over queries, $j \in \{0,\ldots,3\}$ over prompt conditions, $k \in \{1,2\}$ over evidence levels, and $r \in \{1,\ldots,n\}$ over the $n$ repetitions of each cell.
\end{itemize}

\chapter{Preliminaries}

Chapter 1 established that the system prompt is the interface through which a
RAG system's intended behaviour is specified, and asked how systematically
varying that interface changes system behaviour and outcomes. Answering that
question formally first requires the components of a RAG system and the reasons
behind its design. Section 2.1 traces how retrieval-augmented approaches to
language generation developed. Section 2.2 introduces the four components used
throughout the later chapters. Section 2.3 contrasts retrieval with fine-tuning,
Section 2.4 gives the settings in which RAG is typically deployed, and Section 2.5
reviews the advanced architectures this thesis deliberately does not use.

\section{Path towards RAG}
The need to retrieve from an external store instead of remembering everything
internally led to the first question-answering systems. Early systems relied on
hand-crafted rules and pattern matching over the input question, and mapped recognised syntactic patterns onto retrieval or answer-generation procedures
\citep{pandya2021qasurvey}. Statistical and later neural methods replaced those
rule-based components. The dominant approach shifted toward learning the
mapping from question to answer from data. Large language models are
the end point of that shift since they generate answers from parameters learned over
large text corpora.

Large language models generate text using only parametric knowledge, and these are 
information encoded in their weights during training
\citep{wang2024knowledgemechanisms}. This leads to two problems. First, a model's
knowledge is fixed at the point its training data was collected. Any Information after that point is unknown to the model unless it is
retrained or fine-tuned. Second, purely parametric models are prone to
hallucination, because they can generate plausible-sounding statements that no
evidence supports, since nothing in their design forces an output to trace
back to a verifiable source.

Both problems are the reason to give a language model access to an knowledge base at inference time, instead of relying only on what
is stored in its parameters \citep{lin2025llminference}. \citet{lewis2020rag}
formalised this idea as Retrieval-Augmented Generation. RAG combines a retriever, which selects relevant passages from a document
collection, with a pretrained generator, which conditions its output on those retrieved passages in addition to the input query.

RAG has developed beyond that original design since it was introduced.
\citet{gao2023survey} describe three stages: Naive RAG, which runs retrieval and
generation as one fixed pipeline; Advanced RAG, which adds steps such as query
reformulation or re-ranking of retrieved passages before generation; and Modular
RAG, which makes retrieval, generation, and other steps separate
components that can be rearranged for different tasks. Retrieval and generation stay separate steps in all three. 
\section{Components of RAG}

A RAG system has four components. The knowledge base the evidence comes from,
the retriever that selects relevant evidence for a given query, the generator
that produces a response from the query and the retrieved evidence, and the
system prompt that specifies how the generator should behave. This section
introduces each component. Chapter 4 treats them formally.
Figure~\ref{fig:rag} shows how they fit together and which information passes between
them.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{\input{figures/rag_architecture.tex}}
\caption{Architecture of a RAG system showing the four components introduced. The retrieval stage matches the user query against a vector database and returns the top-$K$ chunks. The generation stage combines those chunks with the user query and the system prompt into a single prompt and produces the answer.}
\label{fig:rag}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Knowledge Base }

The knowledge base $D = \{d_1, d_2, \ldots, d_N\}$ is the collection of documents
the evidence comes from. In most RAG systems, documents are first split
into smaller passages, called chunks. Chunking is needed because a language
model can only take in a limited amount of context. 

An embedding model maps each chunk to a high-dimensional vector, so that
chunks with similar meaning end up close together in the embedding space.
Representing text as vectors and measuring relatedness by geometric proximity
goes back to the vector space model of \citet{salton1975vectorspace}.
Similarity between a query vector and a chunk vector is usually measured by the
cosine of the angle between them.

The embeddings are stored in a vector database. This database supports fast
nearest-neighbour search over large collections of chunks. Comparing a query
against every stored vector is exact but becomes too slow as the collection
grows. Therefore vector databases index the vectors and use approximate nearest-neighbour search. The most widely used index of this kind is the hierarchical navigable
small world graph of \citet{malkov2020hnsw}.

Several systems build on such indexes. \citet{pan2024vectordb} survey how they
differ in indexing, query processing and storage. What matters here is how they
are deployed. FAISS is a library embedded directly in an application, with
fine-grained control over the index structure \citep{johnson2021faiss}. Chroma
is a lightweight database aimed at local development and small deployments. The
\texttt{pgvector} extension adds vector columns and similarity operators to
PostgreSQL, so that vectors can sit next to existing relational data. Pinecone is a
managed service in which the provider handles indexing and scaling.
\subsection{Retriever}

Given a user query $q$ and a knowledge base $D$, the retriever selects the chunks
that are relevant for answering $q$. Chapter 4 formalises this as a probability
distribution $p_\eta(z \mid q,D)$ over relevant documents. Two approaches are
common and match a query to a passage in different ways.
\subsubsection{Dense Retrieval}

Dense retrieval embeds the query with the same embedding model used for the
knowledge base and ranks chunks by the similarity between the query vector and
each chunk vector. Because the embedding is learned, two passages that express
the same content in different words end up close together. Hence, a query can match
a chunk with which it shares no vocabulary. This is the mechanism the original
RAG architecture of \citet{lewis2020rag} relies on.

\subsubsection{Sparse Retrieval}

Sparse retrieval scores chunks by term overlap with the query. The standard
method is BM25 \citep{robertson2009bm25}. BM25 weights each matching term by how
often it occurs in the chunk, how rare it is across the collection, and how long
the chunk is. Common words therefore contribute little, and long chunks are not
favoured only for containing more words. The representation is sparse since
a chunk is described by the vocabulary it contains. BM25 requires no training and remains a strong baseline.

\subsubsection{Retrieval Depth}

Whichever method is used, the top-$K$ ranked chunks form the retrieved context.
Retrieval quality depends on both how many chunks are retrieved and how precise
the ranking is. Too few chunks risk missing needed evidence, while too many risk
adding irrelevant information to the generation step. The order of
the chunks also matters. \citet{liu2024lostmiddle} report that answer
accuracy depends on where the relevant passage sits within the context. This
thesis fixes the retriever, the chunking and $K$ across all experimental
conditions. Therefore, changes in the output cannot be traced back to retrieval.

\subsection{Generator}
\label{sec:generator}

The generator is the large language model that produces the final response. Such
a model is a neural network trained on a large text corpus to predict the next
token in a sequence. It generates text by autoregressive decoding. Its parameters, written
$\theta$ in Chapter 4, encode what the model learned during training. This
encoded knowledge is often called parametric memory.

Within a RAG pipeline the model reads the retrieved chunks, selects the parts that answer the query,
resolves references between them, and writes the result as fluent text
\citep{lewis2020rag}.

In practice the query, the retrieved context and the system prompt are not passed
to the model separately. A fixed template combines them into a single input
sequence with the system prompt first so that it shapes how everything
after it is read. Chapter 4 formalises the generator as
$p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S)$, the probability of the next token given the
query, a retrieved document, the tokens already produced and the system prompt.
Because these three together determine the model's input, changing any one of
them can change the output. The others must therefore be held fixed if a change
in behaviour is to be attributed to the system prompt alone.

\subsubsection{Decoding and the Temperature Parameter}

Generation is not deterministic by default. At each step the model produces a
score $u_v$ for every token $v$ in the vocabulary $\mathcal{V}$, and the softmax
function, with a temperature parameter $T > 0$, turns these scores into a
probability distribution:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:temperature}
p(v) = \frac{\exp(u_v / T)}{\sum_{v' \in \mathcal{V}} \exp(u_{v'} / T)}.
\end{equation}

The temperature rescales the scores before they are normalised, and it controls
how much probability mass is left for tokens the model considers less likely
\citep{holtzman2020degeneration}. As $T$ approaches zero the distribution
concentrates on the highest-scoring token, and decoding becomes effectively
deterministic. As $T$ grows the
distribution flattens, lower-scoring tokens become reachable, and the output
becomes more varied across runs.

For open-ended writing this variety is useful. For a RAG system it is mostly harmful. A token that the model scores as unlikely given the retrieved context
is one that the evidence does not support well. Consequently, raising the
temperature increases the chance of producing a statement the retrieved documents
do not contain. High temperature also makes the system harder to evaluate, since
the same query and the same evidence no longer result in the same answer. RAG systems
intended to be faithful to their sources are therefore usually run at a low
temperature. This thesis holds $T$ fixed across all experimental conditions, but
fixes it at the API default of $1.0$ in Equation~\eqref{eq:temperature}. The noise that remains at that setting is the
reason each condition is repeated several times in Chapter 5, and its cost is
stated in Section~\ref{sec:limits}.
\subsection{System Prompt}

The system prompt $S$ specifies the instructions, constraints and output
requirements the generator should follow. The query changes with every request, and the retrieved context changes
with the query and the knowledge base. The system prompt is usually fixed across
many requests.

The system prompt has two functions. The first is to define the role the RAG system occupies, the audience it addresses,
and the format its output takes. The second is to
install hard constraints on that behaviour, of which the grounding constraint is
the most important. A grounding constraint restricts the model to the
retrieved context and specifies what to do when that context does not suffice. Listing~\ref{lst:prompt} illustrates both in a minimal example.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={A minimal system prompt combining an operational scope with two hard constraints.},label={lst:prompt}]
You are a customer service assistant for a parcel delivery company.

GROUNDING RULE
Answer only from the context provided below. Do not use information
from your training data, even where you are confident that it is
correct.

INSUFFICIENT EVIDENCE
If the context does not contain the answer, reply with exactly:
The provided documents do not contain this information.

FORMAT
Answer in at most three sentences. Cite the identifier of every
document you use, in square brackets.
\end{lstlisting}

The first line fixes the operational scope. The two blocks that follow are hard
constraints. One restricts the admissible source of fact, the other defines the
response for the case in which no admissible source exists. The final block
constrains form. Each of
these instructions is stated so that it can be checked against an output. That is
the property Chapter 4 requires of the requirement set $\Sigma$.
\section{Retrieval-Augmented Generation versus Fine-Tuning}
\label{sec:finetuning}

Fine-tuning is the other established way of adapting a general-purpose language
model to a specific application. It is worth stating how it differs from
retrieval before Section~\ref{sec:usecase} turns to use cases. Fine-tuning
continues training on domain-specific data and changes the model parameters
$\theta$, consequently what the model learns enters the parametric memory described in
Section~\ref{sec:generator}. RAG leaves $\theta$ untouched and changes what is
placed in the context window at inference time. The two therefore act on
different parts of the system instead of competing to answer one question. Fine-tuning requires a training run over the
model's parameters whenever the underlying information changes. Adding a document
to a knowledge base requires only that the new chunks be embedded and indexed \citep{cheerla2025enterpriserag}. 
\citet{wang2024knowledgemechanisms} survey the difficulty of locating and editing
individual facts inside model parameters. This matters wherever information is
corrected or retracted, or must be deleted on request. It is also one reason
retrieval is preferred in regulated settings, where sensitive data can remain in
a controlled external store instead of entering the parameters
\citep{he2025biomedicinerag}. Because a RAG answer is
produced from a specific set of retrieved chunks, each claim in the output can in
principle be checked against the passage that supports it \citep{niu2024ragtruth}.

\section{Use-Case of RAG}
\label{sec:usecase}

RAG systems are typically used where the needed knowledge is too large, too
specific, or changes too often to be captured reliably by a model's parameters. The fields of use span many domains: question-answering over internal document collections \citep{cheerla2025enterpriserag},
customer support systems that must answer from current product or policy
documentation, coding assistants that retrieve from a specific codebase instead
of relying on general programming knowledge, legal question-answering over case
law and contract repositories \citep{pipitone2024legalbenchrag}, and clinical
decision support that grounds its answers in current medical literature and
institutional protocols \citep{he2025biomedicinerag}. 
\section{Advanced RAG Architectures}
\label{sec:advanced}

The architecture described so far retrieves once per query and generates once
from what was retrieved. Recent work relaxes that procedure, and the resulting
variants can be grouped by which stage of the pipeline they modify. The summary
below is given for completeness. None of these variants is used here, because this
thesis studies the standard architecture.

\begin{description}
    \item[Conversational RAG.] Retaining prior turns of a dialogue as a state, such that a follow-up question can be answered.
    \item[Pre-retrieval optimisation.] Rewriting or expanding the query before it reaches the retriever. Hypothetical Document Embeddings generate a hypothetical answer to the query and embed that instead of the query itself, based on the rationale that an answer lies closer in embedding space to the passage containing it than a question does \citep{gao2023hyde}.
    \item[Routing and adaptive retrieval.] Deciding where and how much to retrieve. Adaptive-RAG varies the depth of retrieval with the difficulty of the query, answering simple questions directly and reserving multi-step retrieval for those that need it \citep{jeong2024adaptiverag}.
    \item[Self-correction and reflection.] Evaluating retrieved evidence before or during generation. Corrective RAG grades each retrieved document, decomposes the ones it keeps into smaller knowledge strips, discards the strips that are not relevant, and falls back to a wider search when retrieval fails \citep{yan2024crag}. Self-RAG instead trains the model to decide for itself when to retrieve and to critique its own draft output, so that retrieval and generation Intertwine \citep{asai2024selfrag}.
    \item[Agentic RAG.] Treating retrieval as one action among several available to an agent. A meta-agent delegates to document-specific or tool-specific agents and combines their results, which turns a fixed pipeline into a workflow whose steps are chosen at run time \citep{singh2025agentic}.
\end{description}

Understanding the standard architecture
formalised in this thesis remains a prerequisite for all advanced RAG architectures. Each variant
changes the retrieval function $R$, the generation function $G$, or the number of
times they are applied, but all of them are still built from a retrieval step and
a generation step, and all of them still produce a distribution over outputs.

This thesis therefore implements the simplest architecture and none of the variants above.
The reason is methodological as the research objective is to estimate the causal
effect of the system prompt on the output, that Chapter 4 formalises as the
intervention $\mathrm{do}(S = S_j)$. This particular effect is identifiable only as long as
the system prompt reaches the answer through a single path. Each of the advanced variants
breaks that condition. Under Self-RAG the model decides for itself when to
retrieve \citep{asai2024selfrag}, thus the system prompt influences which documents
are fetched as well as how they are used. Under routing, corrective and agentic
designs the prompt shapes the decisions of the router, the retrieval evaluator or
the meta-agent, so that the retrieved context $C$ ceases to be determined by the query
and the corpus alone. Retrieval would then have to be written
$p_\eta(z \mid q,D,S)$ rather than $p_\eta(z \mid q,D)$, and the arrow from $S$
into the retriever that Figure~\ref{fig:causal} deliberately omits would be
present. 

\chapter{Literature Review}

Chapter 2 introduced the components of a RAG system. This chapter reviews three
areas of literature relevant to studying how the system prompt affects such a
system. Section 3.1 covers prompt sensitivity and instruction-following in
language models generally. Section 3.2 turns to retrieval-augmented generation,
its architecture, evaluation, and known failure modes, and Section 3.3 examines
adversarial and competing instructions delivered through retrieved content.
Section 3.4 brings the three together into the research gap this thesis addresses.

\section{Prompt Sensitivity and Instruction-Following in Language Models}
\label{sec:promptlit}

The first branch of literature studies language models with no retrieval
component at all. \citet{cheng2026systemprompts} vary
the system prompt given to instruction-tuned models for code generation, crossing
prompt specificity and prompting strategy with model, programming language and
decoding settings. They report that the system prompt influences the quality of
generated code independently of the task description. Theirs is the closest
published design to the one used here, and the present experiment carries it from
code generation into a retrieval-augmented setting, where the model is also given
evidence to work from. What it varies, however, is specificity and strategy: how
much the prompt asks for and how it asks the model to go about the task. In the
terms of Section~\ref{sec:promptreq} that is variation in $\Sigma$, the requirement
set, and their design therefore leaves open the question this thesis asks, which
is what happens when $\Sigma$ is held fixed and only the wording $\varphi$ changes.
\citet{mu2025robustness} examine how reliably models follow a system prompt when the
user turn contains instructions conflicting with it, and report that the system
prompt does not always retain authority in that competition. Their result cuts
both ways for the present design: it establishes the system prompt as a channel
with measurable influence over behaviour, and it shows that the influence is not
guaranteed. \citet{zheng2024helpful} assign personas
through the system prompt across a large set of roles and find that doing so does
not reliably improve accuracy over one carrying no persona at all.

A larger body of research varies the user-side prompt. \citet{lu2024sensitivity} compare
prompting techniques for in-context learning and measure sensitivity through
gradient-based saliency scores. They report that sensitivity correlates
negatively with accuracy and can serve as an unsupervised proxy for it.
\citet{zhuo2024prosa} propose ProSA, which scores instance-level variation in the
task instruction against decoding confidence, and report that larger models are
more robust to such variation and that few-shot examples reduce it.
\citet{salinas2024butterfly} perturb task prompts across text classification
tasks (adding a trailing space, requesting the output in XML, or prefixing a
jailbreak) and find that perturbations of this size change the labels the model
assigns. \citet{sclar2024quantifying} vary only the formatting of few-shot task
templates (separators, casing and spacing, none of which carries propositional
content) and report differences of up to 76 accuracy points between formats a
reader would judge equivalent. \citet{hua2025flaw} run twelve task templates
across several benchmarks and argue that much of the measured sensitivity is
produced by the evaluation method (log-likelihood scoring and rigid answer
matching) rather than by the model, since the variance falls once an LLM judge is
used instead. \citet{qin2026interactions} decompose a model's output score into
interactions between input variables and observe that small changes to the prompt
destabilise those interactions even where the final output is unchanged.

Two further studies fall outside both groups. \citet{ruiz2026promptcarefully} state
a rule in the instruction given to a model and then shift the contingencies that
rule was written for.
The channel carrying the rule is not the variable of interest there, but the
finding bears on how a stated requirement competes with what the evidence in front
of the model supports. \citet{ying2024intuitive} study conflict between a model's
parametric memory and the evidence supplied in its context, and classify models as
intuitive or dependent by which source they favour. That conflict runs between
memory and context, not between a system prompt and a user instruction, so the
study bears on the evidence axis of this thesis rather than on its prompt axis.

Two conclusions follow. The first is that the
system prompt is a behavioural control variable whose effect can be isolated and
measured given a properly controlled setup, which the three system-prompt studies
support directly \citep{cheng2026systemprompts,mu2025robustness,zheng2024helpful}.
The second is that instructions are sensitive to changes that preserve their
meaning, whether lexical
\citep{lu2024sensitivity,zhuo2024prosa,salinas2024butterfly}, purely
formatting-level \citep{sclar2024quantifying}, or not visible in the output at all
\citep{qin2026interactions}, and that the apparent size of such an effect depends
on how it is measured \citep{hua2025flaw}. This second conclusion is established
for the user-side prompt, and its transfer to the system prompt is an assumption
instead of a result. The assumption is reasonable, since both texts reach the
same decoder as tokens in the same context window, but it is untested.

\section{Retrieval-Augmented Generation: Architecture, Evaluation, and Failure Modes}

The second branch is closer to retrieval-augmented generation.

\citet{lewis2020rag} introduce Retrieval-Augmented Generation, combining a
pretrained neural retriever with a pretrained sequence-to-sequence generator, and
show that conditioning generation on retrieved evidence outperforms purely
parametric models on knowledge-intensive tasks. Their probabilistic formulation,
in which retrieval is a distribution over documents and the retrieved document is
marginalised out, is the direct basis for the model formalised in Chapter 4.
\citet{gao2023survey} survey the development of RAG systems through Naive,
Advanced and Modular paradigms. Their taxonomy supports treating
retrieval and generation as separable functions whose interaction with the system
prompt can be studied independently, as this thesis does. Two later developments
extend this taxonomy in directions this thesis deliberately does not follow.
\citet{edge2024graphrag} restructure the retrieval side into a knowledge graph,
grouped into thematic segments and pre-summarised by an LLM, so that a query is
answered by traversing entities and summaries. \citet{singh2025agentic} and
\citet{jin2025searchr1} restructure the generation side into an agent loop, in
which the model itself decides when and what to retrieve, calling $R$ repeatedly
and adaptively; \citet{jin2025searchr1} train this
behaviour directly with reinforcement learning and report improvements over
standard RAG baselines. Both directions relax exactly the components Chapter 4
holds fixed: $R$ in the first case, and the single-call structure of $G$ in the
second. For that reason this thesis retains \citeauthor{gao2023survey}'s simplest,
Naive-RAG form, so that any change in the outcomes measured here can be attributed to
the system prompt rather than to the added architectural complexity of a
graph-structured retriever or an agentic generation loop.

\citet{es2023ragas} propose Ragas. It is a reference-free evaluation framework for RAG
pipelines built around three metrics that need no ground-truth human annotation:
faithfulness, answer relevance and context relevance. This thesis adopts faithfulness and answer relevance. The third, context
relevance, is a retrieval-side measure and is excluded for the reasons given in
Section~\ref{sec:metrics}. Their framework also motivates evaluating the effect of
the system prompt independently of retrieval quality. \citet{chen2024rgb}
construct the Retrieval-Augmented Generation Benchmark (RGB) and show that current
language models tolerate a moderate amount of retrieval noise but struggle with
negative rejection, with information integration across multiple retrieved
passages, and with robustness to counterfactual or contradictory evidence. Two of their four test environments shape the design of this thesis.
\citet{niu2024ragtruth} introduce RAGTruth, a manually annotated corpus of
word-level hallucinations produced by RAG systems, and benchmark
hallucination-detection methods across multiple language models. Their finding
that hallucination persists even when relevant evidence has been retrieved
motivates measuring faithfulness as an outcome distinct from retrieval quality, as
this thesis does.

Taken together, these studies show that a RAG system's output quality depends on
both retrieval quality and on how faithfully the generator uses the evidence it
was given \citep{es2023ragas,niu2024ragtruth}. Current systems
already struggle with conflicting or insufficient
retrieved evidence, even without any prompt manipulation \citep{chen2024rgb}.
None of the studies in this branch, however, treats the system prompt as a
controlled experimental variable. It is either fixed across conditions or not
specified at all.

\section{Adversarial and Competing Instructions in Retrieved Content}

A third line of work concerns instructions that arrive in the retrieved content rather than in the system prompt, and that compete with it for control of the model's behaviour. The first branch shows that a model's own system prompt can lose authority to instructions arriving in the user turn \citep{mu2025robustness}; \citet{greshake2023notwhat} show that text hidden inside a document the model is told to process can override the system prompt altogether. \citet{zhang2025poisoning} build a RAG-specific benchmark on this basis, covering 13 poisoning attack methods and 7 defenses across 5 base question-answering datasets and 10 expanded variants. \citet{chen2024rgb} and \citet{niu2024ragtruth} in the second branch already find that retrieved evidence is often noisy, incomplete, or contradictory, so competing content reaches the generator even when no attacker is involved. The work in this branch, however, treats instruction competition exclusively as a security problem: an adversary deliberately crafts malicious content. The goal is to detect or defend against it. None of them examines benign linguistic variation in the system prompt itself, the kind the prompt-sensitivity literature reviewed above studies outside of RAG, and the kind this thesis' conditions are built to isolate inside it. The question here is therefore not whether a RAG system can be attacked, but whether ordinary, well-intentioned rewordings of its own instructions change its behaviour at all.

\section{Synthesis and Research Gap}

The first branch shows two things: that the system prompt is a controllable way of shaping language model behaviour, and that instructions are sensitive to rewording that leaves their semantic meaning unchanged. It establishes the controllability result for the system prompt, the sensitivity result mostly for the user-side prompt, and both outside any retrieval-augmented setting. The second branch establishes the architecture, evaluation vocabulary, and known failure modes of RAG systems. The third branch shows that instructions can compete with the system prompt in RAG systems and that this competition is actively studied, but only as a deliberate security threat delivered through retrieved content, never as benign, controlled linguistic variation of the system prompt itself. The overlap between all three branches is still largely unexamined.

This thesis treats the system prompt as a controlled experimental variable inside a RAG pipeline, holding the query, knowledge base, retrieval mechanism, and underlying language model fixed, so that any change in the system's behaviour and outcomes can be traced to the prompt itself. Every condition varies only the wording of the instructions and leaves what they require untouched, which separates the effect of phrasing from the effect of content.

Chapter 4 builds the formal model that makes this control precise. It describes the RAG system as a retrieval function combined with a generation function, with the system prompt as an explicit parameter of the generation function, and states the assignment of a prompt condition as an intervention on that model.

\chapter{Formal Model of a RAG System}

Chapter 3 identified the research gap: prompt sensitivity is studied outside retrieval, and RAG evaluation does not treat the system prompt as a controlled variable. Closing that gap requires a model of the system precise enough to isolate the system prompt. This chapter builds that model on the probabilistic formulation of retrieval-augmented generation given by \citet{lewis2020rag}, and extends it by making the system prompt an explicit variable of the generation process.

\section{Modelling Objective}

The formalisation does two things. It states the probabilistic relationship between the user query, the retrieved documents and the generated output. It also provides the basis for treating the system prompt as an experimental intervention whose effect can be measured while the query, the knowledge base, the retriever and the language model are held constant.

The model can be read as a chain,
\[
(q,D) \;\longrightarrow\; p_\eta(z \mid q,D) \;\longrightarrow\; p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S) \;\longrightarrow\; p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S),
\]
in which the system prompt $S$ enters at the generation stage only. The research question of this thesis concerns that final distribution: what changes in $p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S)$ when $S$ is changed?

The symbols used below are those listed in the Notation section of Chapter 1. The one departure from \citet{lewis2020rag} is that they write the input sequence as $x$, whereas this thesis writes it as $q$, since the input is always a user query. Their notation is otherwise kept unchanged.

\section{Probabilistic Retrieval}

Retrieval is represented as a probability distribution over documents rather than as a single choice:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:retrieval}
p_\eta(z \mid q,D).
\end{equation}

Equation~\eqref{eq:retrieval} reads as the probability the retriever assigns to document $z$, given the query $q$ and the knowledge base $D$. Writing retrieval this way means the retriever never has to commit to one document being the correct one. It spreads probability over the candidates, and documents it considers more relevant receive more of it.

A knowledge base may hold far more documents than can be evaluated in practice. The formulation therefore keeps only the $K$ documents with the highest retrieval probability,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:topk}
\mathcal{Z}_K(q,D) = \operatorname*{TopK}_{z \in D} \; p_\eta(z \mid q,D),
\end{equation}

and the retrieved context handed to the generator is the resulting collection,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:context}
C = \{z_1,\ldots,z_K\}, \qquad z_k \in \mathcal{Z}_K(q,D).
\end{equation}

The distinction between $z$ and $C$ in Equation~\eqref{eq:context} matters later. The first is a candidate the model sums over, the second the concrete collection the running system retrieves.

\section{Prompt-Conditioned Generation}

\citet{lewis2020rag} write the generator at the level of a single token as $p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1})$: the probability of the next token given the query, one retrieved document, and the tokens already produced. This thesis adds the system prompt as a further conditioning variable:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:token}
p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S).
\end{equation}

Equation~\eqref{eq:token} is the point at which this thesis departs from the original formulation. The generator is the same model with the same parameters $\theta$; the system prompt is now written into the conditioning information.

Because generation is autoregressive, the probability of a complete output sequence follows from the token probabilities by multiplication:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:sequence}
p_\theta(y \mid q,z,S) = \prod_{i=1}^{L} p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S).
\end{equation}

\section{Marginalizing over Retrieved Documents}
\label{sec:ragdist}

Equation~\eqref{eq:sequence} gives the probability of an output under document $z$. Since the system does not know which retrieved document supports the answer, the model averages over the candidates, weighting each by its retrieval probability. \citet{lewis2020rag} give two ways of doing this, which differ only in where the sum is placed.

\subsection{RAG-Sequence}

RAG-Sequence assumes that a single retrieved document is responsible for the whole answer. The probability of an output is the weighted average, over documents, of the probability that document would produce that entire sequence:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ragseq}
p_{\text{RAG-Sequence}}(y \mid q,D,S) \;\approx\; \sum_{z \in \mathcal{Z}_K(q,D)} p_\eta(z \mid q,D) \prod_{i=1}^{L} p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S).
\end{equation}

The sum stands outside the product, and that is what encodes the assumption: one document is fixed, the entire sequence is generated from it, and only then is the next document considered.

\subsection{RAG-Token}

RAG-Token makes the weaker assumption that different parts of an answer may come from different documents. The average over documents is taken separately at every token:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ragtok}
p_{\text{RAG-Token}}(y \mid q,D,S) \;\approx\; \prod_{i=1}^{L} \sum_{z \in \mathcal{Z}_K(q,D)} p_\eta(z \mid q,D) \, p_\theta(y_i \mid q,z,y_{1:i-1},S).
\end{equation}

The whole difference between the two is the order of the operators: $\sum_z \prod_i$ against $\prod_i \sum_z$. RAG-Sequence commits to one document for the length of the answer; RAG-Token lets one document supply the first half of a sentence and another the second.

Equations~\eqref{eq:ragseq} and~\eqref{eq:ragtok} differ only in the order of the two operators. Where the distinction does not matter, both are written here as $p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S)$, the distribution the Notation section of Chapter 1 names. It does not matter for this experiment: the system prompt $S$ appears in exactly the same place in both, inside the generator and nowhere else, so nothing that follows depends on which of the two is assumed.

\section{The System Prompt: Requirements and Wording}
\label{sec:promptreq}

The system prompt enters Equation~\eqref{eq:token} as a single symbol, but it is a text with internal structure, and the experiment varies that structure deliberately. A prompt states a set of requirements and states them in particular words. Writing the two separately gives

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:prompt}
S = \rho(\Sigma,\varphi), \qquad \Sigma = \{\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_m\},
\end{equation}

which separates what a prompt asks for from the words it asks in. Two prompts built from the same $\Sigma$ ask for the same thing. Two built from the same $\varphi$ say things the same way.

The conditions cross two factors. The first is the requirement set. Three are used, each taken from a prompt published with a retrieval-augmented system and reproduced in Section~\ref{sec:conditions}. Writing $\Sigma_A$, $\Sigma_B$ and $\Sigma_C$ for the three: $\Sigma_A$ is the response-generation prompt of \citet{friel2024ragbench}, which states a single requirement; $\Sigma_B$ is the VANILLA instruction of \citet{gao2023alce}, which states about ten clauses falling into four independently checkable families; and $\Sigma_C$ is the instruction of \citet{mao2024fitrag}, which states three. The three sets therefore differ by roughly an order of magnitude in how much they ask for, which is what lets the wording effect be separated from the density of the prompt it acts on.

The second factor is the wording. Four wordings are written for each requirement set, holding $\Sigma$ fixed and changing only $\varphi$. The dimensions along which the wording is varied are taken from three prior studies rather than devised here. \citet{wahle2024paraphrase} vary prompts by linguistically defined paraphrase type rather than by ad hoc rewriting, and group those types into families, of which morphology, syntax and lexicon apply to a standing instruction. \citet{zhan2024lexical} isolate the lexical axis by substituting single semantically similar words, and report that models are over-sensitive to variations imperceptible to a reader. \citet{kostic2026samemeaning} separate a lexical pipeline built on synonym substitution from a syntactic one built on dependency parsing, and find that the two behave differently since lexical perturbation degrades performance consistently, syntactic perturbation does not.

This thesis follows their approach of varying a prompt along linguistically defined, meaning-preserving dimension one at a time, and applies it to the standing system prompt of a retrieval-augmented system. Its research question requires that application, and none of the three studies addresses it. Writing $S_{b,j}$ for the $j$-th wording of baseline $b$:
\begin{description}
    \item[$S_{b,0} = \rho(\Sigma_b,\varphi_0)$, canonical.] The published form, unchanged. The reference the other three are measured against.
    \item[$S_{b,1} = \rho(\Sigma_b,\varphi_1)$, lexical.] Synonym substitution with clause order and voice held fixed.
    \item[$S_{b,2} = \rho(\Sigma_b,\varphi_2)$, syntactic.] Clause reordering and active to passive, with the vocabulary held as far as English allows.
    \item[$S_{b,3} = \rho(\Sigma_b,\varphi_3)$, format.] The same wording laid out as a list rather than as prose.

\end{description}

Twelve conditions follow. Each axis changes one thing and holds the rest fixed. That is what makes a difference between two conditions attributable to that axis. The syntactic variant carries obligation uniformly through the construction \textit{is to be}, so that a change of voice does not also change how strongly something is required; the format variant writes its items as bare noun phrases for the same reason. No condition strengthens or weakens an obligation, because that would change $\Sigma$ rather than $\varphi$.

Baseline $A$ states the fewest requirements and is short enough to give in full. The four conditions read:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={The four conditions of baseline $A$, whose canonical form is the prompt of \citet{friel2024ragbench}.},label={lst:baselineA}]
A0  Use the following pieces of context to answer the question.

A1  Employ the following excerpts of material to address the query.

A2  The question is to be answered using the context pieces below.

A3  CONTEXT: the following pieces
    TASK: use them to answer the question
\end{lstlisting}

The variants for baselines $B$ and $C$ are written on the same principle and are longer in proportion to their requirement sets. Within each baseline the four conditions are held to within one tenth of the canonical form in length, so that a difference between two of them cannot be read as an effect of how much text the prompt contains rather than of the axis being varied.

Crossing the wording dimension with three requirement sets rather than one is what lets the research question be asked properly. If the system responded only to what a prompt requires and not to how it is written, $S_{b,0}$ through $S_{b,3}$ would produce the same output distribution within every baseline, since they differ in $\varphi$ alone. Whether they do is the experimental question. The format condition in particular follows \citet{sclar2024quantifying}, who report large performance differences from formatting changes of this kind. They apply those changes to few-shot task templates rather than to a system prompt, so carrying the manipulation across to $S$ is itself part of what is tested here. Using three baselines also answers an objection a single one cannot. An effect found with one requirement set might be a quirk of that particular prompt rather than a property of rewording. Three sets, differing in how much they ask for, show whether the same sensitivity appears in more than one place.

Every contrast is taken within a baseline. Comparing $S_{B,1}$ against $S_{A,0}$ would confound a change of wording with a change in what is asked for, so each variant is measured against the canonical form of its own requirement set.

\section{The System Prompt as an Intervention}
\label{sec:intervention}

The prompt appears in the generator (Equation~\eqref{eq:token}) and not in the retriever (Equation~\eqref{eq:retrieval}). The retrieval term is therefore written without $S$ in its arguments: the system prompt is supplied to the generator but plays no part in choosing documents. In the notation of \citet{pearl2009causality}, $S$ has an effect on $y$ and no effect on $z$. Figure~\ref{fig:causal} shows the resulting structure.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[
    box/.style   = {draw, rounded corners=2pt, minimum height=9mm, minimum width=21mm,
                    align=center, font=\small},
    tx/.style    = {draw, very thick, rounded corners=2pt, minimum height=9mm,
                    minimum width=21mm, align=center, font=\small},
    ar/.style    = {-{Latex[length=2mm]}},
    lb/.style    = {font=\scriptsize, inner sep=2pt, fill=white, align=center}
]
\node[box] (q)    at (0,1.4)    {$q$};
\node[box] (d)    at (0,-1.4)   {$D$};
\node[box] (retr) at (3.9,-1.4) {Retriever $\eta$};
\node[box] (z)    at (7.6,-1.4) {$z$};
\node[tx]  (s)    at (7.6,2.4)  {$S$};
\node[box] (gen)  at (11.3,0)   {Generator $\theta$};
\node[tx]  (y)    at (14.9,0)   {$y$};
\node[box] (m)    at (18.4,0)   {$M(Y)$};

\draw[ar] (q) -- (retr);
\draw[ar] (d) -- (retr);
\draw[ar] (retr) -- (z);
\draw[ar] (z) -- (gen);
\draw[ar] (q) -- (gen);
\draw[ar, very thick] (s) -- (gen);
\draw[ar] (gen) -- (y);
\draw[ar] (y) -- (m);
\end{tikzpicture}%
}
\caption{The system prompt $S$ and the output $y$ are drawn in bold. $S$ enters the generator and has no arrow into the retriever, which is what allows a change in $y$ to be attributed to a change in $S$.}
\label{fig:causal}
\end{figure}

For the effect of the prompt to be interpretable, the following are held constant across conditions. Each query is used unchanged in every condition it is run in, so that every comparison is made within a query rather than between queries, and the knowledge base is fixed at $D = D_0$. The retriever and its parameters are fixed at $\eta = \eta_0$, and the language model at $\theta = \theta_0$, so the model is never changed.

What is deliberately held fixed is the generator's output distribution. Although $\theta$ stays fixed, the conditional distribution it defines may differ between $S_0$ and $S_j$: that difference is the effect the experiment is built to measure. Decoding is also stochastic, so the same configuration can produce different outputs from one run to the next. The term $\varepsilon$ captures that variation:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:noise}
Y = G(q,z,S;\theta,\varepsilon),
\end{equation}

which is why each condition is repeated several times.

\section{The Causal Effect}

Setting the system prompt to a fixed value is an intervention, written $\mathrm{do}(S = S_j)$ following \citet{pearl2009causality}. The notation separates \textit{setting} a variable from merely \textit{observing} it. For a query $q$ under prompt condition $S_j$, the output of the system is the random variable
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:potential}
Y \sim p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(y \mid q,D,S_j).
\end{equation}

For one query the effect of the prompt is the difference in the expected value of the measured property $M(Y)$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:potential} between the two conditions. That difference is not, however, the quantity of interest. A prompt that improves the answer to one question and degrades the answer to the next has established no effect at all, and a difference observed on a single input cannot be separated from the particular difficulty, phrasing or subject matter of that input. The research question asks about the behaviour of the system over the queries it will actually meet, so the effect has to be defined as an average over the distribution from which queries are drawn. Writing $P_{\mathcal{Q}}$ for that distribution over the query space $\mathcal{Q}$, the causal effect of prompt condition $S_j$ relative to the baseline $S_0$ is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:estimand}
\tau_j \;=\; \mathbb{E}_{q \sim P_{\mathcal{Q}}}\Bigl[\; \mathbb{E}_{Y \sim p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(\cdot \mid q,D,S_j)}\bigl[M(Y)\bigr] \;-\; \mathbb{E}_{Y \sim p_{\mathrm{RAG}}(\cdot \mid q,D,S_0)}\bigl[M(Y)\bigr] \;\Bigr],
\end{equation}

which connects the probability model of Section~\ref{sec:ragdist} directly to the quantity the experiment estimates. Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand} is what Chapter 5 measures.

Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand} is an average treatment effect in the standard sense: the query plays the role of the unit, the prompt condition the role of the treatment, and $\tau_j$ the mean over units of the difference between the two treatments. Two features of how it is written matter. The difference is taken inside the outer expectation. That is what makes each query its own control, and it matches the paired design of Section~\ref{sec:intervention}. By linearity of expectation the two forms agree, but only the form given here records the pairing. The expectations are also nested rather than pooled: the inner expectation averages over the randomness of retrieval and decoding at a fixed query, while the outer averages over the queries themselves, so a large effect confined to a handful of inputs is not mistaken for a general one.

The distribution $P_{\mathcal{Q}}$ is not known and cannot be written down. Chapter 5 replaces it with the empirical distribution of a query set. That is the point at which the choice of data becomes a claim about generality instead of a matter of convenience, and it is why Section~\ref{sec:knowledgebase} draws the queries from established question-answer datasets.

The choice of $M$ is left open here. Section~\ref{sec:goodanswer} describes what that property has to capture, and Section~\ref{sec:metrics} fixes it.

\section{Grounding the Knowledge Base in Established Datasets}
\label{sec:knowledgebase}

The knowledge base has so far been written as a set $D = \{d_1,\ldots,d_N\}$. Filling $D$ is a methodological decision rather than a further step of formalisation. It is settled here because the effect defined in Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand} is only as informative as the corpus and the queries it is estimated over.

The route taken here is to instantiate $D$ and the query set from an established question-answer dataset. The corpus is public, versioned and split in a documented way, so a third party can repeat the experiment. And because published results for a wide range of retrieval and generation systems already exist on these datasets, the effect estimated in Chapter 5 can be read against a background of prior work.

The collection is built from queries sampled from Bing search logs, with candidate passages retrieved for each query and free-form answers written by human judges \citep{nguyen2016msmarco}. MS MARCO provides the queries $q$, the documents that populate $D$, and, through its annotations, both the reference answers $A_q$ and the fact set against which an output can be judged.

What it does not provide is a definition of a good answer. An annotation records which answer is correct; it says nothing about whether an answer is grounded in the evidence actually retrieved, whether it addresses the question directly, or whether it obeys the requirements the system prompt states. Those properties are the object of this thesis, and defining them is the subject of the next section.
\section{What Counts as a Good Answer}
\label{sec:goodanswer}
Quality in a retrieval-augmented system is not one property but several, and they can fail independently of one another. The first is groundedness. An answer should rest on the documents the retriever actually supplied. The second is relevance. An answer should address the question that was asked and address it directly, so that hedging, padding and drift towards a neighbouring question all count as failures. The third is correctness. An answer should agree with what is in fact the case, which is a different demand from the first and is treated separately here for the reason given below. The fourth is obedience. Whatever the system prompt asks for should be visible in the output itself, whether in length, in form, or in the handling of missing evidence, since a prompt that is not followed cannot be said to have any effect on quality at all.

Groundedness and correctness are kept apart deliberately, because collapsing them would conceal the failure this thesis is concerned with. The retrieved context is neither complete nor guaranteed to be free of error. An answer can therefore be correct while ignoring the evidence entirely, drawing instead on what the generator already holds in its parameters, and it can use the evidence faithfully and still be wrong because the evidence was wrong. A system that scored well on correctness alone would reveal nothing about whether retrieval was used at all, which is precisely the behaviour a system prompt is supposed to govern.

All four are properties of the text the generator produces. Each of the four is estimated by a measure taken from published work. Groundedness by faithfulness, which asks whether every claim can be traced to a retrieved passage; relevance by answer relevance, which asks whether the response addresses the question put to it; correctness by agreement with the reference answer recorded in the data; and obedience by whether the output satisfies every instruction the prompt states.

The formal definitions this evaluation rests on are introduced in Chapter 5. That chapter states the set of acceptable answers precisely and fixes the measures that estimate the four properties. There are five measures for the four, because correctness is measured twice: faithfulness for groundedness, answer relevance for relevance, ROUGE-L and BLEU-1 for correctness, and instruction adherence for obedience. Each of the five is used in turn as the measured property $M$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand}, so the effect of a prompt condition on each is estimated separately. Chapter 5 also specifies the corpus, the query set, the experimental conditions and the procedure by which those effects are estimated.

\chapter{Experimental Setup}

Chapter 4 built a model of the RAG system and defined the effect of a prompt condition as the average treatment effect $\tau_j$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand}. This chapter turns that model into a concrete experiment. It states the research question and the hypothesis, describes the system that is tested and which of its parts are held fixed, defines the prompt conditions, explains how the data are collected, and fixes how the outputs are scored and analysed. All experiments are implemented in Python. The implementation follows the structure of this chapter, and every design decision stated here is checked by the code before any data are collected.

\section{Research Question and Hypothesis}
\label{sec:goal}

Chapter 1 asked to what extent systematic changes in the system prompt influence the behaviour and outcomes of a RAG system when the user input, the knowledge base, the retrieval mechanism and the language model are held fixed. The experiment puts that question in the form it can be tested in: \textit{does a RAG system behave differently when the structure of its system prompt is changed while the meaning of the prompt stays the same?}

Structure means how the prompt is written: which words are used, how the sentences are built, and how the text is laid out on the page. Meaning means the set of requirements $\Sigma$ that the prompt states, in the sense of Equation~\eqref{eq:prompt}. Every condition of the experiment keeps $\Sigma$ fixed and changes only the wording $\varphi$. If the system responded only to what a prompt requires and not to how it is written, all wordings of one prompt would produce the same distribution of answers. Whether they do is the empirical question. Stated as hypotheses:

\begin{description}
    \item[$H_0$.] System prompts that state the same requirements in different wordings produce the same distribution of answer quality: $\tau_j = 0$ for every rewording $j$, in every baseline, on every measure and at both evidence levels.
    \item[$H_1$.] At least one rewording changes answer quality: $\tau_j \neq 0$ for some rewording in some baseline on some measure at some evidence level.
\end{description}

The effect $\tau_j$ is defined within a cell. It depends on the baseline whose canonical form the rewording is measured against, on the measure the quality is read with, and on the evidence level the comparison is made at, as well as on the wording $j$ itself. Carrying all four indices would make every expression below unreadable, so only $j$ is written and the other three are fixed by the surrounding text; Section~\ref{sec:analysis} states them explicitly wherever an estimate is given.

The comparison is always between a reworded prompt $S_{b,j}$ and the canonical prompt $S_{b,0}$ of the same baseline, on the same queries and with the same retrieved evidence. What is measured is the change in answer quality, where quality is the four properties of Section~\ref{sec:goodanswer}: grounded, relevant, correct and obedient.

One secondary question is asked of the same experimental data. Does the answer to the main question survive when the quality of the evidence changes? The evidence is therefore supplied at two levels, as a controlled factor rather than as a further treatment (Section~\ref{sec:variables}).

\section{Overview of the Experiment}
\label{sec:overview}

The system under test is the simple RAG architecture of Chapter 2. It is used for the reason given in Section~\ref{sec:advanced}: in this architecture the system prompt reaches the answer along one path, through the generator, so its effect can be identified. A query is embedded, the $K$ most similar passages are retrieved from a vector index, and a language model produces the answer from the system prompt, the passages and the query. Table~\ref{tab:fixed} lists the components of that system and the value each one is fixed at.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{The components of the system under test and their fixed values. None of them changes between prompt conditions.}
\label{tab:fixed}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Component & Fixed value \\
\midrule
Knowledge base $D$ & MS MARCO v2.1 validation passages, $N = 122{,}678$ \\
Chunking $\chi$ & one passage per chunk, as published \\
Embedding model & \texttt{all-mpnet-base-v2}, mean pooling, L2-normalised \\
Vector index & FAISS flat inner-product index, exact search \\
Retrieval depth $K$ & 10 \\
Generator $\theta$ & \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} via the chat completions API \\
Decoding & temperature $1.0$ (the API default), at most 256 new tokens \\
Judge & \texttt{gpt-4.1-mini}, temperature $0$, fixed prompts \\
Query set & 100 MS MARCO queries, seed 20260904 \\
Repetitions $n$ & 3 per cell \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The experiment has three stages, of which the first two collect data. In the first stage, retrieval is run once for every query and its result is written to disk. In the second stage, every prompt condition is applied to every query, reading the stored retrieval result, and the answers are written to disk. In the third stage, the answers are scored and the prompt effect is estimated. Section~\ref{sec:procedure} describes the first two stages in detail and Section~\ref{sec:metrics} the third.

\section{System-Prompt Conditions}
\label{sec:conditions}

The system prompt is the only thing that changes between conditions. A condition is a pair of a baseline and a wording. This section first introduces the three baselines and then the four wordings each baseline is written in.

\subsection{Three Published Baselines}

The prompts are not written for this experiment. Each baseline is a system prompt published with a retrieval-augmented system. The three sources state different numbers of requirements. That is why three are used rather than one. A prompt with one requirement gives a change of wording little to act on. A prompt with ten requirements is long, and a wording effect found on it alone could be an effect of length. 

Baseline $A$ is the response-generation prompt of \citet{friel2024ragbench}, reproduced in Listing~\ref{lst:minimal}. It states one requirement: answer from the supplied context.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={Baseline $A$: the response-generation prompt of \citet{friel2024ragbench}.},label={lst:minimal}]
Use the following pieces of context to answer the question.
{documents}
Question: {question}
\end{lstlisting}

Baseline $B$ is the VANILLA instruction of \citet{gao2023alce}. It constrains accuracy, engagement, concision and register, it fixes the syntax and the density of citations, and it warns that some of the supplied results may be irrelevant.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={Baseline $B$: the VANILLA instruction of \citet{gao2023alce}, reproduced from their Table 23.},label={lst:alce}]
Write an accurate, engaging, and concise answer for the given
question using only the provided search results (some of which
might be irrelevant) and cite them properly. Use an unbiased and
journalistic tone. Always cite for any factual claim. When citing
several search results, use [1][2][3]. Cite at least one document
and at most three documents in each sentence. If multiple documents
support the sentence, only cite a minimum sufficient subset of the
documents.
\end{lstlisting}

Baseline $C$ is the instruction of \citet{mao2024fitrag}. Its authors describe it as three parts: the first directs the model to the retrieved passages, the second asks it to read the question carefully, and the third asks for an answer together with a justification. 

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={Baseline $C$: the comprehensive instruction of \citet{mao2024fitrag}.},label={lst:fitrag}]
Refer to the passage below and answer the following question.
Make sure you fully understand the meaning of the question and
passages. Then give the answer and explain why you choose this
answer.
Passages: {passages}
Question: {question}
\end{lstlisting}

The published prompts contain placeholders for the documents and the question. In this experiment the instruction text alone forms the system prompt $S$ and is sent in the system role of the chat interface. The passages and the question are sent in the user role, in the fixed template of Listing~\ref{lst:userturn}. The template is the same in every condition, so the only text that differs between conditions is $S$. Passages are numbered from one, so that a citation instruction has something to refer to and so that the position of a passage can be recovered from the answer.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={The user turn. It is identical in every condition; only the system turn $S$ changes. With no passages, the line after \texttt{Passages:} reads \texttt{(none)}.},label={lst:userturn}]
Passages:
[1] <first passage>
[2] <second passage>
...

Question: <the query>
Answer:
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Four Wordings of Each Baseline}

Each baseline is written in four wordings. The first is the canonical form above. The other three each change one aspect of the wording and leave everything else as it is. The dimension follow the paraphrase types of \citet{wahle2024paraphrase}, \citet{zhan2024lexical} and \citet{kostic2026samemeaning}, as explained in Section~\ref{sec:promptreq}. Table~\ref{tab:wordings} states what each wording changes and the rule it follows.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{The four wordings. Every wording of a baseline states the same requirement set $\Sigma_b$.}
\label{tab:wordings}
\begin{tabular}{llp{7.6cm}}
\toprule
$j$ & Wording & What changes, and the rule kept \\
\midrule
0 & canonical & Nothing. The published form. \\
1 & lexical & Words are replaced by synonyms. Clause order and voice are kept. \\
2 & syntactic & Clauses are reordered and active voice becomes passive. The vocabulary is kept as far as English allows. Obligation is carried uniformly by \textit{is to be}, so that the change of voice does not change how strongly something is required. \\
3 & format & The same wording is laid out as a list with headings instead of as prose. List items are bare noun phrases, so that no obligation is added or removed. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

A rewording that changed how strongly an instruction is stated, for example by turning \textit{always cite} into \textit{please try to cite}, would change what the prompt requires. Such a change is a change of $\Sigma$, and a difference in behaviour caused by it could not be read as an effect of structure. No condition of this experiment makes a change of that kind.

The four wordings of baseline $A$ are given in Listing~\ref{lst:baselineA} in Chapter 4. Listings~\ref{lst:baselineB} and \ref{lst:baselineC} give the three rewordings of baselines $B$ and $C$; their canonical forms are Listings~\ref{lst:alce} and \ref{lst:fitrag} above.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={The lexical ($B1$), syntactic ($B2$) and format ($B3$) wordings of baseline $B$.},label={lst:baselineB}]
B1  Compose a precise, compelling, and succinct reply for the posed
    query drawing only on the supplied search results (a number of
    which may be immaterial) and reference them correctly. Adopt an
    impartial and journalistic register. Invariably reference every
    factual assertion. When referencing several search results,
    employ [1][2][3]. Reference at minimum one document and at
    maximum three documents per sentence. If several documents
    corroborate the sentence, only reference a minimal adequate
    subset.

B2  For the given question an accurate, engaging, and concise answer
    is to be written from the provided search results alone, some
    of which might be irrelevant, and cited properly. An unbiased,
    journalistic tone is to be used. A citation is always given for
    any factual claim. Where several results are cited, [1][2][3]
    is used. In each sentence at least one and at most three
    documents are cited. Where multiple documents support a
    sentence, only a minimum sufficient subset is cited.

B3  ANSWER
    - accurate, engaging, and concise, for the given question
    - source: only the provided search results
    - note: some of the provided results might be irrelevant

    CITATIONS
    - cite the search results properly
    - always cite for any factual claim
    - when citing several search results: [1][2][3]
    - in each sentence: at least one document, at most three documents
    - if multiple documents support the sentence: only a minimum
      sufficient subset

    TONE
    - unbiased and journalistic
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox,caption={The lexical ($C1$), syntactic ($C2$) and format ($C3$) wordings of baseline $C$.},label={lst:baselineC}]
C1  Consult the excerpt below and respond to the following query.
    Ensure you completely grasp the sense of the query and excerpts.
    Then supply the response and justify why you select this
    response.

C2  The following question is to be answered from the passage below.
    The meaning of the question and passages is first understood.
    The answer is then given, with an explanation of why it is
    chosen.

C3  SOURCE: the passage below, and nothing else
    READ: fully understand the meaning of the question and passages
    ANSWER: give the answer to the following question
    EXPLAIN: say why you choose this answer
\end{lstlisting}

Prompt length is held nearly constant within a baseline, so that a difference between two wordings cannot be an effect of how much text the prompt contains. Table~\ref{tab:lengths} gives the word counts. No wording departs from its canonical form by more than one tenth, and the code refuses to run if an edit to a prompt breaks that bound.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Word count of every condition, with the deviation from the canonical form of its baseline.}
\label{tab:lengths}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
Baseline & canonical & lexical & syntactic & format \\
\midrule
$A$ & 10 & 10 ($\pm 0\%$) & 11 ($+10\%$) & 11 ($+10\%$) \\
$B$ & 74 & 71 ($-4\%$) & 80 ($+8\%$) & 77 ($+4\%$) \\
$C$ & 33 & 32 ($-3\%$) & 34 ($+3\%$) & 32 ($-3\%$) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Twelve conditions result, three baselines in four wordings each. They are written $S_{b,j}$ with $b \in \{A,B,C\}$ and $j \in \{0,1,2,3\}$. Every comparison is made within a baseline. Comparing $S_{B,1}$ with $S_{A,0}$ would mix a change of wording with a change of what is asked for, so each rewording is compared only with the canonical form of its own baseline.

\section{Variables and Controlled Factors}
\label{sec:variables}

The experiment has one independent variable, one dependent variable, one controlled factor that is varied for robustness, and a set of components that are held constant.

\paragraph{Independent variable.} The wording $j$ of the system prompt, with four levels, nested within the baseline $b$. Changing $j$ changes the structure of the prompt and nothing else. The requirement set $\Sigma_b$ is the same at every level of $j$.

\paragraph{Dependent variable.} The quality of the answer, as scored by the measures of Section~\ref{sec:metrics}: faithfulness, answer relevance, correctness and instruction adherence, with correctness measured twice, by ROUGE-L and by BLEU-1. Each of the five is used in turn as the measured property $M$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand}, so the effect of a rewording is estimated five times, once per measure. The five are reported separately and are not combined into a single score, for the reason given in Section~\ref{sec:analysis}.

\paragraph{Controlled factor.}  The context $C$ handed to the generator is set directly, as the intervention $\mathrm{do}(C = c_{ik})$ of Chapter 4, at two levels:
\begin{description}
    \item[$c^{\text{gold}}$.] Only the passages the annotation marks as supporting the answer. The evidence needed for the answer is certainly present, so a failure here belongs to the generator rather than to retrieval. Every query is usable at this level, which makes it the more sensitive of the two.
    \item[$c^{\text{ret}}$.] The $K$ passages the fixed retriever actually returns. This is the system as it would be deployed.
\end{description}

The retrieval depth is not a second factor. It is held constant at $K = 10$. That depth admits more of the annotated evidence than a shallower one, as Table~\ref{tab:gate} shows, so more queries are usable at the retrieved level and the comparison there rests on a larger sample; and ten passages give the prompt a longer and noisier context to govern than fewer would. 

\paragraph{Held constant.} Everything in Table~\ref{tab:fixed}. The generator is one model behind one API, with a fixed temperature and a fixed output limit. Queries and passages are encoded by one embedding model, applied to both sides in the manner of the dense retriever of \citet{karpukhin2020dpr}. The index, built with the library of \citet{johnson2021faiss}, is constructed once. Each passage is one chunk. Because the embedding model, the index, the chunking and the depth are all fixed, the set $\mathcal{Z}_K(q,D)$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:topk} depends on the query alone and never on the system prompt. The context is held identical in a stronger sense than that. Not only the set of passages but also their order are the same in every condition since they are determined by the query, the evidence level and the repetition only.

\paragraph{Covariates.} Two properties of each query are accounted for and can be conditioned on in the analysis. The category (direct or multi-hop) and MS MARCO's intent type. Both are defined in Section~\ref{sec:corpus}.

\paragraph{The design.} An observation is one answer, written
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:design}
y_{ijkr} \;\sim\; p_{\mathrm{RAG}}\bigl(y \;\big|\; q_i,\ \mathrm{do}(C = c_{ik}),\ \mathrm{do}(S = S_j)\bigr), \qquad r = 1,\ldots,n,
\end{equation}
in Equation~\eqref{eq:design}, for query $i$, prompt condition $j$, evidence level $k$ and repetition $r$. The twelve prompt conditions and the two evidence levels are fully crossed, which gives 24 cells. Every cell is run for all 100 queries and repeated $n = 3$ times, which is 7,200 generations before the retrieval gate of Section~\ref{sec:procedure} removes any.

Within a cell the only remaining source of variation is the decoding randomness $\varepsilon$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:noise}. Each repetition is an independent draw from the same distribution, so averaging $n$ of them leaves the quantity being estimated unchanged and reduces the standard error of the cell mean by a factor of $\sqrt{n}$. Three repetitions is fewer than the five \citet{zhuo2024prosa} use for the same purpose, and the cost of that choice is stated in Section~\ref{sec:limits}.

\section{Knowledge Base and Query Set}
\label{sec:corpus}

Following Section~\ref{sec:knowledgebase}, both the knowledge base and the query set come from MS MARCO \citep{nguyen2016msmarco}, read from the Hugging Face export of version 2.1. The validation split is used. It holds 101,093 queries, of which 12,467 carry a non-empty \texttt{wellFormedAnswers} field. Only those rows are used, because the well-formed answers are the references against which correctness is measured, and a query without one cannot be scored.

\paragraph{The knowledge base.} The corpus holds every passage attached to any of the 12,467 rows. MS MARCO ships about ten candidate passages per query, retrieved by Bing and then judged by annotators, so the corpus contains the gold evidence for every query. Each passage is one chunk and is used at the length at which it was published. The median passage has about 50 words and three sentences, eight in ten passages have between two and six sentences.

\paragraph{The query set.} $N_Q = 100$ queries are sampled from the same 12,467 rows. They fall into two categories. A row is \textit{direct} when exactly one of its passages is marked \texttt{is\_selected}, and \textit{multi-hop} when two or more are. Fifty queries are drawn from each category.

\begin{description}
    \item[$\mathcal{Q}_{\text{direct}}$.] The answer is carried by a single passage. 
    \item[$\mathcal{Q}_{\text{multi-hop}}$.] The human-written answer draws on two or more passages.
\end{description}

Within each category the draw is balanced across MS MARCO's five intent types, \textsc{description}, \textsc{entity}, \textsc{location}, \textsc{numeric} and \textsc{person}, ten queries per type. The natural distribution is very uneven: on the well-formed rows \textsc{description} makes up 44\% and \textsc{person} 7\%. Balancing makes the type usable as a covariate. The draw uses the seed 20260904 and is reproducible from the same split.

The multi-hop half is drawn from a much smaller pool than the direct half. Of the 12,467 well-formed rows, 12,062 are direct and only 386 are multi-hop; 19 have no passage marked at all and are dropped. The thinnest cell, multi-hop \textsc{person}, holds 27 candidates for the ten drawn. The multi-hop sample is therefore a substantial fraction of what exists.

\section{Experimental Procedure}
\label{sec:procedure}

The experiment runs in two stages. Retrieval is finished and written to disk. Generation reads that stored output. This separation is what enforces the assumption of Section~\ref{sec:intervention} that the system prompt has no effect on which documents are retrieved.

\subsection{Stage 1: Retrieval and the Retrieval Gate}

Queries and passages are encoded with \texttt{all-mpnet-base-v2}, using mean pooling over the last layer and L2 normalisation, so that the inner product of two vectors is their cosine similarity. The index is a flat inner-product index: every query is compared against every passage, with no approximation. At 122,678 vectors of 768 dimensions the index occupies 0.38~GB and fits in memory, and exactness matters more than speed here, because an approximate index would make retrieval depend on how the index was built instead on the query alone.

Retrieval is run once, at $K = 10$, for all 100 queries. The identifiers, scores and ranks of the returned passages are written to disk.

The retrieval stage is also where the evidence is checked. A query passes the \textit{retrieval gate} when every passage its annotation marks as supporting appears among the $K = 10$ returned. For a direct query that is one passage, where as for a multi-hop query it is every passage the answer draws on. Table~\ref{tab:gate} reports the result. It is a property of the fixed retriever and the corpus, not of any prompt condition, and it is reported before the results because it sets the denominator of the retrieved level.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{The retrieval gate at the depth used, $K = 10$. A query passes when all of its gold passages are among the ten returned. Gold recall counts the gold passages that arrived, over all 152 gold passages of the query set. The figures for $K = 5$ are given for comparison; that depth is not run.}
\label{tab:gate}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
$K$ & Passed & direct & multi-hop & Gold recall \\
\midrule
$10$ (used) & 88 / 100 & 45 / 50 & 43 / 50 & 139 / 152 (91.4\%) \\
5 & 74 / 100 & 41 / 50 & 33 / 50 & 123 / 152 (80.9\%) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Queries that fail the gate are excluded from the retrieved level. The measures of Section~\ref{sec:metrics} are not defined on evidence that was never supplied. When the context lacks the passage carrying the answer, a correct answer can only have come from the generator's memory, and an incorrect answer cannot be told apart from a faithful reading of the other passages. Scoring such a cell would measure the retriever and report the result as an effect of the prompt. 

\subsection{Stage 2: Generation}

For every query, every cell of the design is run $n = 3$ times. The cells are visited in a random order per query, so that any drift in the API over the collection period cannot align with a condition. Every generation is an independent request. The context for a cell is built as follows.

\begin{itemize}
    \item At $c^{\text{gold}}$, the passages are the query's gold passages.
    \item At $c^{\text{ret}}$, the passages are the top $K$ from the stored retrieval output.
\end{itemize}

The order of the passages within the context is shuffled, and the positions of the gold passages are recorded, because answer accuracy depends on where the relevant passage sits \citep{liu2024lostmiddle}. The system prompt $S_{b,j}$ is sent in the system role and the passages and question in the user role, in the template of Listing~\ref{lst:userturn}. The generator is \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} at temperature 1.0 with at most 256 new tokens.

For each generation the following are written to disk: the query and its category and type; the condition, evidence level, depth and repetition; the identifiers and order of the passages in the context and the positions of the gold passages; the exact system prompt; the model identifier, the decoding settings and the backend fingerprint the API returned; the answer and its token count; and a timestamp.

\subsection{The Pipeline}

The whole procedure, in order:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Download the MS MARCO v2.1 validation split and keep the 12,467 rows with a well-formed answer.
    \item Embed every passage attached to those rows and build the index: 122,678 passages.
    \item Sample the query set: 50 direct and 50 multi-hop queries, ten per intent type, seed 20260904.
    \item Retrieve the top 10 passages for every query and apply the retrieval gate. The top 5 are retrieved as well, so that the gate can be reported at that depth for comparison in Table~\ref{tab:gate}, but no generation is run at that depth.
    \item For every query, every one of the 24 cells and every one of the 3 repetitions, build the context, send $S_{b,j}$ and the user turn to the generator, and record the answer.
    \item Score every answer with the five measures of Section~\ref{sec:metrics}.
    \item Estimate the effect of each rewording in every cell, once per measure, with a confidence interval.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Evaluation}
\label{sec:metrics}

Many different texts can express the same answer, so the standard defines a set of acceptable answers. An answer belongs to that set when it satisfies four requirements at once:

\begin{description}
    \item[Grounded.] It asserts only what the supplied passages state, and introduces nothing from outside them.
    \item[Relevant.] It addresses the question that was asked, and addresses it directly. 
    \item[Correct.] It agrees with the reference answer of the dataset annotation.
    \item[Obedient.] It satisfies every requirement the system prompt states.
\end{description}

An answer that breaks none of the four is a good answer. One that breaks several is worse than one that breaks fewer. Each requirement is estimated by one measure, except correctness, which is estimated by two. All of them are taken from published evaluation work. The first three are the measures that the survey of \citet{yu2024ragevalsurvey} names as the evaluation targets of the generation component: faithfulness relates the response to the retrieved documents, answer relevance relates it to the query, and correctness relates it to the reference answer. Retrieval measures are excluded by construction, because the system prompt cannot change what is retrieved.

\subsection{Five Measures for Four Properties}

\paragraph{Faithfulness.} Whether every claim in the answer can be traced to a supplied passage. It is scored reference-free, after \citet{es2023ragas}, in two steps. First the judge (Section~\ref{sec:judge}) breaks the answer into atomic factual claims. Then, for each claim, the judge decides whether the passages state it or directly include it. The breach count $n_1(y)$ is the number of unsupported claims, the reported rate is the supported fraction since a raw count grows with answer length and prompts change how much a model writes. This is the measure the experiment relies on. Every one of the twelve conditions states a grounding constraint, and faithfulness shows whether that constraint is followed in practice. Because the passages are identical across conditions, a change in faithfulness cannot come from a change in what the generator was given.

\paragraph{Answer relevance.} Whether the answer addresses the question actually asked. A prompt can degrade an answer without making it false. Relevance is scored reference-free, following \citet{es2023ragas}: the judge writes three questions that the answer would be a good answer to, and those are compared with the question actually asked by their mean cosine similarity in the space of the retrieval encoder, such that no second embedding space is introduced. A mean below $0.5$ counts as a breach. Both evidence levels of this design supply passages, so an answer that declines to answer has declined in the presence of evidence and counts as a breach. A refusal is detected by a fixed list of patterns such as \textit{cannot be answered} and \textit{not enough information}.

\paragraph{Correctness.} Whether the answer agrees with the reference answers $A_q$. It is measured with the two metrics MS MARCO's free-form answer is scored with \citep{nguyen2016msmarco}, and that \citet{lewis2020rag} report on it: ROUGE-L \citep{lin2004rouge} and BLEU-1 \citep{papineni2002bleu}. Both compare the answer with the references word by word. Before scoring, the text is lowercased and punctuation is turned into spaces, and citation markers such as \texttt{[3]} are removed from the answer, because baseline $B$ asks for them and they would otherwise lower its score for follow its prompt. ROUGE-L is the F-measure of the longest common subsequence of the answer and a reference: its precision and its recall are each taken at their best over the references and combined with $\beta = 1.2$, which weights recall slightly above precision, as the MS MARCO scorer does. BLEU-1 is the share of the answer's words that also occur in a reference, each word counted at most as often as it appears in any single reference, multiplied by a brevity penalty when the answer is shorter than the reference closest to it in length. The official scorer computes BLEU over the whole corpus; since the estimate averages a value per answer, the per-answer form is used here. Both scores lie between 0 and 1.

Correctness and faithfulness are separate metrics. An answer can repeat a supplied passage accurately and still be wrong about the world, and it can be right about the world while resting on nothing the retriever returned. 

\paragraph{Instruction adherence.} Whether the answer does what the prompt told it to. It is scored as prompt-level strict accuracy in the sense of \citet{zhou2023ifeval}: an answer is adherent only if it satisfies every checked requirement. So the score is binary. For baseline $B$ the rule parses the citation markers and checks that every sentence carries between one and three of them, which is what the prompt asks for. For baseline $C$ the rule checks that the answer contains a justification marker. The markers are the causal connectives \textit{because}, \textit{since} and \textit{therefore}, the phrases \textit{this is why} and \textit{the reason}, and three phrases that point to a source: \textit{as the passage} (or document, or text), \textit{according to} and \textit{based on}. The last group names where an answer came from rather than why it was chosen, so the rule is more forgiving than the requirement it stands for.  Baseline $A$ states only grounding, which faithfulness already measures, so its adherence is undefined by construction. The requirement set is the same for all four wordings of a baseline, so adherence is directly comparable across them.

\subsection{The Judge}
\label{sec:judge}

Faithfulness and answer relevance have no reference string. Therefore, they are scored by a second language model, as \citet{es2023ragas} and \citet{saadfalcon2024ares} do. The judge is \texttt{gpt-4.1-mini}, a different model from the generator, so that the generator never grades its own output. It is run at temperature 0 to ensure that the verdict depends on the answer and the passages and not on a random draw. Since this thesis assumes that language models are sensitive to their instructions, the judge cannot be assumed reliable. Three precautions are taken, following \citet{zheng2023judge}, who document position and verbosity biases in this setting. The judge's own prompts are fixed constants. The judge is shown only the answer and the passages, never the condition label, the query identifier or the evidence level. 

\subsection{Estimating the Effect of a Rewording}
\label{sec:analysis}

The metric reported is the average treatment effect of Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand}. Chapter 4 left the measured quality of the answer $M$ as a placeholder. It is fixed here by the five measures in turn, and therefore, the effect of a rewording is estimated five times. Once for faithfulness, once for answer relevance, once each for ROUGE-L and BLEU-1, and once for instruction adherence. Every measure is oriented such that a higher value is better, and hence, a positive effect therefore means the rewording improved that measure.

For one measure $M$, one evidence level and one baseline $b$, the estimate of the effect of wording $j$ is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:tauhat}
\hat{\tau}_{j} \;=\; \frac{1}{|I|} \sum_{i \in I} \Bigl( \bar{M}_{ij} - \bar{M}_{i0} \Bigr), \qquad \bar{M}_{ij} \;=\; \frac{1}{n} \sum_{r=1}^{n} M\bigl(y_{ijr}\bigr),
\end{equation}
where $I$ is the set of queries in that cell and $\bar{M}_{ij}$ is the mean of the measure over the $n$ repetitions of query $i$ under condition $S_{b,j}$. Equation~\eqref{eq:tauhat} is the sample version of Equation~\eqref{eq:estimand}. The inner average takes the expectation over decoding, and the outer one takes it over queries.

\paragraph{Deciding the hypothesis.}
\label{sec:decision}
$\hat{\tau}_{j}$ is an average over a finite sample and is therefore almost never exactly zero, even when the true effect is. Every estimate is consequently reported with its standard error and a 95\% confidence interval, computed from the paired differences that produced it, and an interval that excludes zero is taken as evidence that the rewording moved that measure.

$H_1$ claims only that at least one rewording changes answer quality, so the claim is assessed against every estimate at once, and at the 5\% level approximately one interval in twenty excludes zero when no effect is present. The intervals are for this reason corrected by the method of \citet{holm1979}, applied within one measure at one evidence level. Each such group forms a \textit{family}, the set of rewordings over which the claim ranges for that measure in that cell. Five measures at two evidence levels yield ten families. Eight of them contain nine rewordings each, three baselines with three rewordings apiece, while the two instruction-adherence families contain six, since adherence is undefined under baseline $A$. $H_0$ is rejected if at least one rewording still moves at least one measure after correction, and is retained if no interval survives. Retaining $H_0$ would be a weaker statement than establishing the effect to be zero, since it would indicate only that the design could not separate an effect from decoding noise.


\chapter{Results and Discussion}
\label{ch:results}

Chapter 5 fixed the experiment. It comprises twelve system prompts, two evidence levels, a fixed retriever and generator, and five measures, correctness being measured twice. This chapter reports the results. It gives the effect of each rewording and states the verdict on the hypothesis of Section~\ref{sec:goal}.


The run produced 6,768 answers. At the gold level all 100 queries are usable, which gives $100 \times 12 \times 3 = 3{,}600$ answers. At the retrieved level 88 of the 100 queries pass the gate of Section~\ref{sec:procedure} at $K = 10$, which gives $88 \times 12 \times 3 = 3{,}168$. Every answer was scored on the five measures of Section~\ref{sec:metrics}, and the two judged measures took 30,636 calls to the judge model.

