\section{The Verdict on the Hypothesis}
\label{sec:verdict}

Eighty-four estimates were made across the five measures of Section~\ref{sec:metrics}, and thirty-five of them exclude zero after correction: eleven on adherence, twenty-three on the two correctness metrics and one on faithfulness. $H_0$ states that prompts stating the same requirements in different wordings produce the same distribution of answer quality, so that $\tau_j = 0$ for every rewording, in every baseline, on every measure and at both evidence levels. That is rejected. $H_1$ claims that at least one rewording changes answer quality, and seven of the nine rewordings do, most of them in both cells.

The rejection does not rest on the single faithfulness result. It rests on instruction adherence, where the effects are large and present in both directions, and on correctness, where they are small but consistent. On both, most effects replicate across the two evidence levels.

That replication also answers the secondary question of Section~\ref{sec:goal}, which asked whether the answer to the main question survives a change in the quality of the evidence. It does. Five of the six rewordings of adherence move it at both evidence levels, and their sizes are close: B1 gains $0.223$ on gold and $0.261$ on the retrieved context, C2 loses $0.457$ and $0.424$. Giving the generator the real output of the retriever, distractors included, rather than only the passages the annotation marks as supporting, does not remove the effect of how the prompt is worded. The correctness effects behave the same way: every rewording with a ROUGE-L effect that survives correction at one level has one at the other.


\section{Summary of Findings}
\label{sec:summary}

This thesis asked how far systematic changes in the system prompt influence the behaviour and outcomes of a RAG system when the user input, the knowledge base, the retrieval mechanism and the underlying language model are held fixed. The findings suggest that they influence it considerably.

Twelve system prompts were built from three published baseline system prompts. Each baseline was written in its canonical published form and three rewordings that change the vocabulary, the sentence structure or the layout. Every wording states the same requirements. Each prompt was run over 100 MS MARCO queries at two evidence levels with three repetitions, and that produced 6,768 answers, and every answer was scored on five measures.

The clearest result is on instruction adherence. Eleven of the twelve intervals exclude zero after correction, with five of the six rewordings moving adherence at both evidence levels. In general, the direction of the effect is not fixed. Every rewording of baseline $B$ scored higher than its published form. The lexical and syntactic rewordings of baseline $C$ scored far lower. On faithfulness and answer relevance no effect was found that survives correction, with one exception in one cell that does not replicate in the other. Correctness, measured by ROUGE-L and BLEU-1, moved as well, in smaller steps. Every rewording of baseline $B$ and the syntactic and format rewordings of $C$ raised it and the lexical rewording of $A$ lowered it. 

Taken together, these results reject $H_0$. Prompts that state the same requirements in different words produced measurably different behaviour from the system tested here. That places the wording of a system prompt among the parameters worth choosing with care.

\paragraph{What the design does not establish.}
\label{sec:limits}
Four limits bound these results. The first is the collection: every figure is conditional on MS MARCO, whose queries are short and mostly factoid, so the effects indicate what may be expected elsewhere without establishing it. The second is the size of effect the design can find. With 88 queries at the retrieved level and 100 at the gold level, a paired comparison at the 5\% level detects a standardised effect of about $0.30$ and $0.28$ respectively, and about $0.39$ and $0.37$ after correction. Three repetitions per cell instead of five, and a decoding temperature of $1.0$ instead of a lower one, both widen the variation within a cell, so a measure that did not move is not thereby shown to be unaffected. The third concerns correctness. The measure fixed before the run was containment of the reference answer, which scored a mean of $0.052$ on MS MARCO's sentence-length references and could not have detected any change. It was replaced by ROUGE-L and BLEU-1 after that result was known, so the replacement was chosen with the data in view. Both metrics reward overlapping words rather than truth and favour answers close in length to the reference, and the correctness effects follow answer length in every baseline. The fourth is the judge. Faithfulness and answer relevance are scored by a language model, the agreement study against human labels was not carried out, and an audit found that its output cap truncated the longest claim lists and that it is not fully deterministic at temperature $0$. The two measures the verdict rests on, correctness and instruction adherence, are computed by program and do not depend on it.


